### Comments
Comments start with '#' and continue to the end of the line. 
They can appear anywhere in the file and should be ignored by the parser.
This allows for adding explanations and notes within the definition file without affecting the logic description.

Example:

# This is a comment explaining the devices section
DEVICES {
    # Define two switches with initial values
    SW1 : SWITCH(initial = 0);
    SW2 : SWITCH(initial = 1);

    # Define two NAND gates with 2 inputs each
    G1 : NAND(inputs = 2);
    G2 : NAND(inputs = 2);
}

# This is a comment explaining the connections section
CONNECT {
    # Connect switches to gate inputs
    SW1 -> G1.I1;
    SW2 -> G2.I2;

    # Connect gates to each other
    G1  -> G2.I1;
    G2  -> G1.I2;
}

# This is a comment explaining the monitors section
MONITOR {
    # Monitor the outputs of both gates
    G1, G2
};

END;   



### General Error Handling:
All errors detected while reading the definition file will be reported with the line number, 
column number, error type, and a short explanation. Where possible, the offending token or signal name will be included. 
The parser will attempt to continue after an error so that multiple errors can be reported from one run.



### Syntax Error Handling
Syntax errors occur when the token sequence does not match the EBNF grammar. 
Examples include missing keywords, missing punctuation, malformed declarations, or unexpected tokens.

When a syntax error is found, the parser will report what was expected and what was actually found. 
It will then recover by skipping tokens until a safe synchronisation symbol is found, usually ;, {, or } depending on context. 
This allows parsing to continue with the next declaration or section.

e.g. G1 : NAND(inputs = 2)
will report: Line 4, column 20: Syntax error: expected ';' after device declaration.



### Semantic Error Handling
Semantic errors occur when the syntax is correct but the meaning is invalid. 
Examples include duplicate device names, unknown device types, missing parameters, 
invalid parameter values, undefined devices in connections, and invalid signal references.

a list of semantic error:
Error condition: Duplicate device name
Detection: When adding a device declaration, check whether the name already exists.
Report: Semantic error: device name G1 is already defined.

Error condition: Undefined device in connection
Detection: When checking a connection, look up both device names in the device table.
Report: Semantic error: device G3 has not been declared.

Error condition: Invalid input port
Detection: Compare the referenced input name against the allowed inputs for that device type.
Report: Semantic error: G1.I5 is not a valid input for a 2-input NAND gate.

Error condition: Input connected more than once
Detection: Store each connected input; report an error if a second connection targets the same input.
Report: Semantic error: input G1.I1 is connected more than once.

Error condition: Missing required input connection
Detection: After all connections are parsed, check every required input for every device.
Report: Semantic error: input G1.I2 is not connected.

Error condition: Monitor is not an output
Detection: Check each monitor signal against the output ports of its device.
Report: Semantic error: G1.I1 cannot be monitored because it is an input.